	1. What is Grouping in TestNG?
Grouping is a feature of TestNG that allows us to categorize test methods into logical groups and execute selected groups instead of running all test methods.

It is especially useful when an automation framework contains hundreds or thousands of test cases.

For example, in an e-commerce application, we might have:


Group	Purpose	Example
Smoke	Verify critical functionality after a new build	Login, checkout, payment
Regression	Verify existing functionality after code changes	Login validation, order history
Sanity	Verify a specific functionality after a small change	Updated payment calculation
Login	Execute all login-related tests	Valid and invalid login
Payment	Execute all payment-related tests	Successful and failed payment

Real-world use: Instead of executing the complete regression suite after every deployment, we can execute only the Smoke group to verify critical functionality.

2. Syntax of Grouping
TestNG provides the groups attribute inside the @Test annotation.

Syntax:
@Test(groups = {"groupName"})
public void testMethod() {

}


Example:
import org.testng.annotations.Test;

public class LoginTest {

    @Test(groups = {"smoke"})
    public void verifyValidLogin() {
        System.out.println("Valid Login");
    }

    @Test(groups = {"regression"})
    public void verifyInvalidLogin() {
        System.out.println("Invalid Login");
    }

    @Test(groups = {"smoke", "regression"})
    public void verifyLogout() {
        System.out.println("Logout");
    }
}
Understanding the example
Test method	Assigned groups
verifyValidLogin()	smoke
verifyInvalidLogin()	regression
verifyLogout()	smoke, regression

Important points:
- A test method can belong to one group.
- A test method can belong to multiple groups.
- A group can contain test methods from multiple classes.
- Grouping does not change the functionality of a test method; it controls test selection.



3. How to Execute Groups Using testng.xml
You can select groups for execution through the testng.xml file.

Syntax:
<groups>
    <run>
        <include name="smoke"/>
    </run>
</groups>


4. Include Multiple Groups
We can include multiple groups in the same XML configuration.
<groups>
    <run>
        <include name="smoke"/>
        <include name="regression"/>
    </run>
</groups>


This executes methods belonging to either smoke or regression.


5. Exclude Groups
We can exclude specific groups using the exclude tag.

Syntax:
<groups>
    <run>
        <include name="regression"/>
        <exclude name="slow"/>
    </run>
</groups>


Meaning:
- Include regression tests.
- Exclude tests belonging to the slow group.
