6. Grouping at Class Level
TestNG also supports assigning groups at the class level.

Syntax:
@Test(groups = {"regression"})
public class EmployeeTest {

    @Test
    public void createEmployee() {
        System.out.println("Create Employee");
    }

    @Test
    public void updateEmployee() {
        System.out.println("Update Employee");
    }
}


Here, both methods inherit the regression group.

If XML includes only regression, both methods will execute.
Important distinction
Class-level grouping applies to test methods in the class. It does not mean that every configuration method in the class becomes a group test.

For example:

For example:
@Test(groups = {"smoke"})
public class EmployeeTest {

    @BeforeMethod
    public void beforeMethod() {
        System.out.println("Before Method");
    }

    @Test
    public void createEmployee() {
        System.out.println("Create Employee");
    }

    @Test
    public void updateEmployee() {
        System.out.println("Update Employee");
    }

    @AfterMethod
    public void afterMethod() {
        System.out.println("After Method");
    }
}


Both test methods belong to smoke. The configuration methods are not themselves smoke-group test methods; 

they run according to their lifecycle and the selected test methods.

7. Combination of Class-Level and Method-Level Grouping
This was one of the important cases we discussed.

Example:
@Test(groups = {"regression"})
public class EmployeeTest {

    @Test(groups = {"smoke"})
    public void createEmployee() {
        System.out.println("Create Employee");
    }

    @Test
    public void updateEmployee() {
        System.out.println("Update Employee");
    }

    @Test(groups = {"sanity"})
    public void deleteEmployee() {
        System.out.println("Delete Employee");
    }
}


Effective groups
Test method	Class-level group	Method-level group	Effective groups
createEmployee()	regression	smoke	regression, smoke
updateEmployee()	regression	None	regression
deleteEmployee()	regression	sanity	regression, sanity

Important: Method-level grouping adds to class-level grouping; it does not replace it.
What happens if XML includes only smoke?


<groups>
    <run>
        <include name="smoke"/>
    </run>
</groups>


Expected result:
Test method	Effective groups	Execution
createEmployee()	regression, smoke	Execute
updateEmployee()	regression	Skip
deleteEmployee()	regression, sanity	Skip

This is because only createEmployee() belongs to the included smoke group.


8. What Happens to Configuration Annotations When Grouping Is Used?
This was another important area from your practical experiment.

Grouping selects test methods, while configuration annotations follow their own lifecycle scopes.

Suppose we have:
@Test(groups = {"smoke"})
public class EmployeeTest {

    @BeforeClass
    public void beforeClass() {
        System.out.println("Before Class");
    }

    @BeforeMethod
    public void beforeMethod() {
        System.out.println("Before Method");
    }

    @Test
    public void test1() {
        System.out.println("Test 1");
    }

    @Test
    public void test2() {
        System.out.println("Test 2");
    }

    @AfterMethod
    public void afterMethod() {
        System.out.println("After Method");
    }

    @AfterClass
    public void afterClass() {
        System.out.println("After Class");
    }
}


If XML includes only smoke, both test methods execute.

Expected output
Before Class

Before Method
Test 1
After Method

Before Method
Test 2
After Method

After Class


Important interview clarification
Do not say that grouping executes configuration methods because they belong to the group.

Instead, say:
Grouping determines which test methods are selected for execution. 
Configuration annotations execute according to their lifecycle scope and applicable TestNG configuration rules.

For example:
- If a class has two smoke methods and one regression method, and only smoke is included, the method-level configurations normally run twice.
- The class-level configurations run once for the class lifecycle.
- Suite-level configurations run once for the suite.
- XML <test>-level configurations run once for the applicable XML <test>.
Configuration methods can have additional dependencies and group-specific configuration settings, so the above describes the normal lifecycle, not every possible configuration scenario.

9. Does Grouping Work With Package Names and Class Names?
Yes. Grouping works whether you specify individual classes or packages in testng.xml.
Option A: Specify individual classes
<suite name="AutomationSuite">

    <test name="SmokeTests">

        <groups>
            <run>
                <include name="smoke"/>
            </run>
        </groups>

        <classes>
            <class name="tests.LoginTest"/>
            <class name="tests.EmployeeTest"/>
            <class name="tests.PaymentTest"/>
        </classes>

    </test>

</suite>


Option B: Specify a package
<suite name="AutomationSuite">

    <test name="SmokeTests">

        <groups>
            <run>
                <include name="smoke"/>
            </run>
        </groups>

        <packages>
            <package name="tests"/>
        </packages>

    </test>

</suite>


Difference
Individual class	Package
Executes selected classes	Discovers classes in the specified package
More control over class selection	Convenient when many classes exist
Uses <classes>	Uses <packages>

Your question about BeforeSuite and AfterSuite:

Changing from package-level inclusion to individual class inclusion does not change the suite lifecycle.

If the same classes are discovered and included, @BeforeSuite and @AfterSuite still execute once for the suite, provided their configuration class/methods are discovered.

They do not execute because of grouping or because a particular class has class-level grouping.


12. Interview Questions and Answers
Q1. What is grouping in TestNG?

Grouping is a TestNG feature that categorizes test methods and allows us to execute selected categories instead of executing all test methods.

Q2. Can a test method belong to multiple groups?

Yes. We can assign multiple groups using an array:
@Test(groups = {"smoke", "regression"})


Q3. How do you execute a specific group?

By using <include> inside the <groups> and <run> tags in testng.xml.

Q4. Can we exclude a group?

Yes. We use <exclude name="groupName"/> inside <run>.

Q5. What happens if we include two groups?

TestNG executes methods belonging to either included group. It uses OR logic.

Q6. Can we assign groups at class level?

Yes. We can use @Test(groups = {"regression"}) at class level. Its group applies to test methods in that class.

Q7. What happens when both class-level and method-level groups are assigned?

The method inherits the class-level group and can have additional method-level groups.

Q8. Does method-level grouping override class-level grouping?

No. Method-level groups are added to class-level groups.

Q9. Do @BeforeMethod and @AfterMethod execute for excluded test methods?

Normally, no. They execute around selected test methods, not excluded ones.

Q10. Does @BeforeClass execute if only one method from the class is selected?

Yes, under normal class execution, it executes once for the class lifecycle, even if only one test method is selected.

Q11. Does @BeforeSuite execute because a class has class-level grouping?

No. It executes because of its suite-level lifecycle, not because of grouping.

Q12. Does grouping work with package-level and individual class-level inclusion in XML?

Yes. Grouping can be used with either approach, provided the relevant test classes are discovered.

Q13. Does grouping control test execution order?

No. Grouping selects test methods. Priority and other TestNG execution mechanisms control ordering.

Q14. If a method belongs to smoke and regression, and only smoke is included, will it execute?

Yes. At least one of its groups is included, and it is not excluded.

Q15. If a method belongs to both included and excluded groups, what happens?

It is excluded. Exclusion takes precedence.



Note:

before , after suite , test , class = methods will be executed regardless of the grouping.
before method and after method will be executed with respect to the test method





Configuration annotation	Basic execution rule
@BeforeSuite / @AfterSuite	Suite-level configuration; not directly tied to an individual test method's group.
@BeforeTest / @AfterTest	XML <test>-level configuration; not directly tied to an individual test method's group.
@BeforeClass / @AfterClass	Class-level configuration; runs when the class has applicable selected test methods.
@BeforeMethod / @AfterMethod	Runs around each applicable test method.


Important distinction:
- Grouping determines which @Test methods are selected.
- @BeforeMethod and @AfterMethod are associated with the selected test methods.
- Suite, XML <test>, and class configurations have broader lifecycle scopes, but don't assume they will always execute regardless of grouping. Configuration discovery, applicability, and alwaysRun can affect execution.
Interview-ready answer
"Grouping is primarily used to control which test methods execute. Suite-level and XML <test>-level configurations operate at their respective lifecycle scopes, while class-level configurations operate around an applicable class execution. Method-level configurations execute around applicable selected test methods."

One more thing: alwaysRun = true can affect configuration-method execution, so remember it as a separate concept rather than assuming every configuration annotation ignores group filtering.
